<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="E-Waste - Endpoint Read and Write Load - native JMeter">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Native JMeter endpoint load plan with shared authentication.
Keep 00 Shared authentication enabled. It uses one thread to log in donor1, donor2, recycler1, recycler2, collector1, collector2, auditor1 and admin1 exactly once per run. The existing credential JSON variables remain unchanged. Optional role sections inside setup may be disabled when no enabled load group needs that role. Do not disable a role needed by a write workflow&apos;s prerequisites.
Shared setup waits auth_setup_delay_ms (default 60000), then spaces logins by auth_login_interval_ms (default 7000). This respects the repository default 10 login/refresh requests per minute per client IP when there is no other auth traffic from that IP. Deployment overrides may require different pacing. Setup failures stop the test; no automatic retries hide 429s.
Business groups 01-28 reuse the matching shared access tokens. Donor/recycler/collector threads alternate the two seeded accounts; auditor and administrator threads use their single seeded accounts. Each HTTP request still executes independently. Each write iteration creates its own batch and command keys. This measures concurrent virtual clients using the seeded identities, not distinct-user/login capacity.
Shared tokens are NOT refreshed automatically. Finish the run within the deployed access-token lifetime (repository default 15 minutes from the first login, including setup time). Token expiry remains a visible HTTP 401. Runtime properties are cleared before setup and after the run; saved credentials and workload variables are never cleared. If setup is skipped, missing tokens appear as MISSING_SHARED_TOKEN.
Authentication groups 29-31 retain their independent sessions and are intended for separate authentication/rate-limit runs. Disable business groups and shared setup when running only groups 29-31. They consume the same per-IP login/refresh rate-limit budget.
Original thread counts, ramp-up, loops, enabled states, URLs and credential values are preserved. Each enabled read group contributes its configured threads; each write group contributes write_threads. Several enabled groups run concurrently. Setup and prerequisite samplers are labelled SETUP; compare only target GET or TARGET rows for endpoint response times, excluding setup logins.
Detail read groups select one visible record once per thread. Empty data fails setup. Evidence download requires evidence_batch_id/evidence_id; frozen-input reads require analytics_batch_id, analytics_source_event_id and analytics_service_token. api_base_url is the direct Go API origin for health/readiness. base_url may use the UI /api/v1 proxy or direct API.
Write groups 18-28 create fresh tagged JMeter-load batches and perform prerequisites before the TARGET request. Matching requires eligible recycler profiles, capacity, Kafka/worker availability and collector scopes. Matching polling is bounded; only interim 404 responses are accepted, and final state must be MATCHED. Earlier target operations leave records at their corresponding lifecycle state for later reconciliation. Evidence upload additionally requires upload_file and upload_mime.
Not included as successful write-load groups: legacy /assignments/{id}/accept (selection already creates ACCEPTED assignments) and /analytics-results (owned by the asynchronous analytics worker). Treatment feeds the actual worker.
View Results Tree and Aggregate Report remain shared at the end. Disable View Results Tree for larger headless runs. No Groovy, plugins or scripted samplers are used. JSON-escape credentials when editing login bodies.
</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.tearDown_on_shutdown">true</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
      </elementProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <Arguments guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="Workload Settings - URL, threads, ramp-up, loops, donors">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="base_url" elementType="Argument">
